---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { site } from "../data/site";
import { ids } from "../lib/seo";

const jsonLd = { "@type": "ProfilePage", mainEntity: { "@id": ids(Astro).person }, dateModified: "2026-09-20" };
---

<Base title="About" description="Who Axel Cedercreutz is, how he works, and the work history behind it: product engineer at Gosta Labs, five years at Twice Commerce, Aalto University, F-liiga floorball." path="/about" ogType="profile" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <section class="section intro">
    <div class="wrap two">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
        <h1 class="h">Hi, I'm Axel. I like owning the whole problem.</h1>
        <div class="prose big">
          <p>I am a product engineer, which in practice means I do the talking-to-users part, the deciding-what-to-build part, the building part and the running-it-in-production part, and I get itchy when I am only allowed one of them.</p>
          <p>I have been building web applications since 2016, all of it in early-stage companies. Five years at Twice Commerce as the engineer who also owned the data platform and the growth experiments, and since 2024 the product engineer for the oncology vertical at Gosta Labs, working directly with clinicians. I have been the only engineer, the data person, the frontend person and the "why is the deploy broken" person, sometimes in the same afternoon. That range is the point: it lets me carry a product from schema to interface without waiting on a handoff. Business first, data second, code in service of both.</p>
          <p>Before software I played floorball in Finland's top league, with a season in Sweden along the way, and it shows. I like teams, I like a plan, and I like being told the score. The hockey-analytics side projects are where the two halves overlap: I would rather measure the game than argue about it.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <aside class="facts">
        <dl>
          <div><dt>Now</dt><dd>Product Engineer at <a href={site.employer.url} rel="noopener">{site.employer.name}</a>, since {site.employer.since}</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Before</dt><dd>{site.previous.name}, formerly {site.previous.formerly}, 2019 – 2024</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Based in</dt><dd>{site.location}</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Languages</dt><dd>Finnish and Swedish, native · English, full professional</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Stack I reach for</dt><dd>TypeScript, Python, SQL, React, Node, FastAPI, Postgres, BigQuery and dbt, whatever the problem actually needs</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Off hours</dt><dd>RinkView, Banger Board, Budgy, and watching a lot of hockey</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Elsewhere</dt><dd><a href={site.github} rel="me noopener">GitHub</a> · <a href={site.linkedin} rel="me noopener">LinkedIn</a></dd></div>
          <div><dt>Say hi</dt><dd><a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a></dd></div>
        </dl>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Work history</p>
        <h2>Where I have been.</h2>
      </div>
      <ol class="timeline">
        <li data-reveal>
          <div class="when mono">Oct 2024 – now</div>
          <div>
            <h3>Product Engineer <span>· {site.employer.name}</span></h3>
            <p>Healthcare AI startup in Helsinki building an AI operating system for oncology teams. I own engineering delivery and product priorities for the oncology vertical: requirements gathered with clinical users, then design, testing, implementation and onboarding. React and TypeScript in front, Node and Python with FastAPI behind. The habit of building alongside the people who use the software is the job.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li data-reveal style="--i:1">
          <div class="when mono">Jun 2019 – Sep 2024</div>
          <div>
            <h3>Software Engineer &amp; Analytics Lead <span>· {site.previous.name}, formerly {site.previous.formerly}</span></h3>
            <p>Circular-commerce platform for rental businesses. Designed, built and maintained the data platform: custom ETL pipelines and a dbt and BigQuery semantic layer that gave the company self-service analysis in Looker and PostHog. Shipped recommendation systems, image recognition and LLM features in TypeScript and React for the beta launch of twice.market. Ran roughly a hundred growth experiments, A/B tests and a freemium trial among them, and nearly doubled the conversion rate from website usage to paying customer between 2022 and January 2024.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li data-reveal style="--i:2">
          <div class="when mono">2016 – now</div>
          <div>
            <h3>Freelance consultant <span>· Cedercreutz Consulting</span></h3>
            <p>Web applications and analytics solutions for client organisations. Among them: contributed to Supabase's migration to server-side PostHog tracking, implementing the integrations and the proofs of concept the team extended across further repositories, and built custom connectors for scheduled database syncs.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li data-reveal style="--i:3">
          <div class="when mono">Jan 2017 – Dec 2019</div>
          <div>
            <h3>Co-founder &amp; advisor <span>· Dash</span></h3>
            <p>Co-founded a student-led design hackathon and advised the organisers who ran it after me.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Education and the rest</p>
        <h2>Where it comes from.</h2>
      </div>
      <ol class="timeline">
        <li data-reveal>
          <div class="when mono">2021 – 2022</div>
          <div>
            <h3>MSc, Computer Science <span>· Aalto University, with honours</span></h3>
            <p>User, Data and Design. Thesis on demand forecasting for access-based consumption: a machine-learning approach to forecasts and inventory recommendations, with an interactive application for stakeholders to explore forecasts and service-level trade-offs. Written while working at Twice Commerce, on Twice Commerce's problem.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li data-reveal style="--i:1">
          <div class="when mono">2016 – 2020</div>
          <div>
            <h3>BSc, Computer Science <span>· Aalto University</span></h3>
            <p>Information Networks: a programme that mixes software, data and business, which turned out to be a fair description of the job.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li data-reveal style="--i:2">
          <div class="when mono">Summer 2016</div>
          <div>
            <h3>Web Development Bootcamp <span>· BrainStation, Toronto</span></h3>
            <p>A summer of full-time web development. This is where the coding started, and it never really stopped.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li data-reveal style="--i:3">
          <div class="when mono">2016 – 2018</div>
          <div>
            <h3>Floorball <span>· F-liiga, Finland's top league</span></h3>
            <p>Two seasons at the top level with Steelers in Hämeenlinna, after a season in Sweden as a junior. Twice-a-day training through high school taught me most of what I know about showing up.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Also</p>
        <h2>Smaller things with public source.</h2>
      </div>
      <ul class="cards">
        <li class="card" data-reveal>
          <p class="eyebrow">2024 · Next.js · Supabase</p>
          <h3>darts-app</h3>
          <p>Darts scoring with authentication, game tracking and statistics, updated live between players through Supabase Realtime.</p>
          <a href="https://github.com/axelcedercreutz/darts-app" rel="noopener">Source on GitHub →</a>
        </li>
        <li class="card" data-reveal style="--i:1">
          <p class="eyebrow">2024 · Express · TypeScript</p>
          <h3>backend-posthog-demo</h3>
          <p>The proof of concept behind the Supabase work: server-only product analytics with PostHog, identity kept in HTTP-only cookies, nothing tracked from the browser.</p>
          <a href="https://github.com/axelcedercreutz/backend-posthog-demo" rel="noopener">Source on GitHub →</a>
        </li>
        <li class="card" data-reveal style="--i:2">
          <p class="eyebrow">2019 – 2022 · Teknologföreningen</p>
          <h3>Student-nation apps</h3>
          <p>A registration system for Teknologföreningen's annual ball, built on Node and PostgreSQL, and later a hand in the digital freshman points booklet, E-Phuxpoängshäfte.</p>
          <a href="https://github.com/axelcedercreutz/arsfest" rel="noopener">Årsfest registration →</a>
          <a href="https://github.com/Teknologforeningen/e-phuxpoangshafte" rel="noopener">E-Phuxpoängshäfte →</a>
        </li>
        <li class="card" data-reveal style="--i:3">
          <p class="eyebrow">2020 – 2021 · Coursework and hackathons</p>
          <h3>The practice reps</h3>
          <p>Aalto's web software development project, the full set of Full Stack Open assignments, and a StupidHack weekend with a Telegram bot that had no business existing.</p>
          <a href="https://github.com/axelcedercreutz/project-self-monitoring" rel="noopener">Self-monitoring app →</a>
          <a href="https://github.com/axelcedercreutz/fullstackopen" rel="noopener">Full Stack Open →</a>
          <a href="https://github.com/axelcedercreutz/stupidhack-2021" rel="noopener">StupidHack 2021 →</a>
        </li>
        <li class="card" data-reveal style="--i:4">
          <p class="eyebrow">GitHub · since 2016</p>
          <h3>Everything else</h3>
          <p>Twenty-odd public repositories, forks and experiments, back to the very first ones from a Toronto summer. Some of it is embarrassing. That is how you know it is real.</p>
          <a href={site.github} rel="me noopener">github.com/axelcedercreutz →</a>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .h { font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 5vw, 4rem); margin: 12px 0 22px; max-width: 16ch; }
  .two { display: grid; gap: 36px 64px; }
  @media (min-width: 900px) { .two { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 3fr) minmax(0, 2fr); align-items: start; } }
  .big { font-size: 1.12rem; }
  .facts { padding: 22px 24px; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius); background: var(--card); position: sticky; top: 88px; }
  .facts dl { display: grid; gap: 16px; margin: 0; }
  .facts dt { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.66rem; letter-spacing: 0.14em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-3); margin-bottom: 4px; }
  .facts dd { margin: 0; font-weight: 600; }
  .timeline { list-style: none; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .timeline li { display: grid; gap: 6px 32px; padding: 24px 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
  @media (min-width: 760px) { .timeline li { grid-template-columns: 200px minmax(0, 1fr); } }
  .when { font-size: 0.78rem; color: var(--ink-3); letter-spacing: 0.03em; padding-top: 6px; }
  .timeline h3 { font-size: 1.25rem; }
  .timeline h3 span { color: var(--ink-3); font-weight: 500; }
  .timeline p { color: var(--ink-2); margin: 6px 0 0; max-width: 62ch; }
  .cards { list-style: none; display: grid; gap: 16px; }
  @media (min-width: 640px) { .cards { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); } }
  @media (min-width: 960px) { .cards { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); } }
  .cards .card { display: grid; gap: 8px; align-content: start; }
  .cards .card > * { min-width: 0; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  .cards h3 { font-size: 1.15rem; }
  .cards p { color: var(--ink-2); margin: 0; font-size: 0.97rem; }
  .cards a { font-weight: 600; text-decoration: none; }
  .cards a:hover { color: var(--ember-ink); }
</style>
